EX={
'glance':['Hanumān has more shrines than almost any other Hindu deity, but most are small and local: a carved slab at a village gate, a roadside niche, a shrine in a police station or a wrestling ground. The great temples here are only the best known.',
 'Northern shrines often call him Bālājī or Mahāvīr; southern ones Āñjaneya or Māruti; the image ranges from the beard of Sālāsar to the giant standing forms of Tamil Nadu.'],
'sankatmochan':['The temple stands near the Assi ghat area, in a grove. Its main image faces Rāma, as tradition says Hanumān always faces his master. Tuesdays and Saturdays see long queues; the evening ārati is famous.',
 'The music festival, held for several nights in April, has hosted many of India’s greatest classical musicians, who perform as an offering. Musicians of different faiths have taken part.',
 'The 2006 blast killed worshippers at the temple and elsewhere in Vārāṇasī. The temple reopened at once, and the mahant’s call for calm was widely noted.'],
'hanumangarhi':['Hanumāngaṛhī is one of Ayodhyā’s oldest and busiest shrines. Its fortress-like walls enclose a courtyard, and the main image of the boy Hanumān on Añjanā’s lap is small and heavily decorated.',
 'The temple is run by Rāmānandī sādhus of the Nirvāṇī Akhāṛā. Since the consecration of the Rāma temple in 2024, pilgrim numbers have grown steeply.'],
'lete':['The reclining image is unusual: most Hanumāns stand or kneel. Local tradition says that after the war, or on his way back, Hanumān lay down here exhausted, and the image marks the spot.',
 'Pilgrims to the Kumbha and Māgha Melas at the Saṅgam usually visit Lete Hanumān as part of the round.'],
'patna':['Mahāvīr Mandir is among the most visited temples in Bihar. It is also known for its social work: the trust runs hospitals and charitable kitchens.',
 'Its laddu, called naivedyam, is made by cooks from Tirupati’s tradition and sold to pilgrims.'],
'jakhu':['The climb to Jakhu from the Ridge in Shimla is steep, and many pilgrims now take a ropeway. The statue, one of the tallest Hanumān images in the world when built, dominates Shimla’s skyline.',
 'Monkeys on Jakhu are notorious for snatching glasses and bags; visitors are told to keep things in pockets.'],
'salasar':['The Sālāsar Bālājī image, with its round face, moustache and beard, is said to have been ploughed up in a field at Asota village in 1754; the local thakur had a dream instructing him to send it to Sālāsar. The image of Hanumān with facial hair is very rare.',
 'Twice-yearly fairs bring hundreds of thousands of pilgrims, many walking from distant towns with flags.'],
'mehandipur':['Mehandipur Bālājī is widely known in North India for rites for people believed to be afflicted by spirits or black magic. Families come for days of prayer and rituals.',
 'Mental-health professionals and rationalist groups have criticised the treatment of distressed people there. We note this as a well-known controversy, without taking sides on belief.'],
'anjaneri':['Añjaneri has an old Jain cave group and Hindu temple ruins at its foot, and the Añjanī Mātā temple partway up. The hill is a common half-day trek from Nashik.',
 'Its birthplace claim rests on the name and the local temple traditions.'],
'anjanadri':['Añjanādri is a short drive from Hampi via Ānegundi. The hilltop temple is simple: a carved image of Hanumān on the rock, with Rāma and Sītā shrines beside it. Pilgrims recite the Cālīsā on the climb.',
 'Karnataka has been developing the hill as a major pilgrimage site, and Hanumān Jayantī brings large crowds. The view over the Tuṅgabhadrā, the boulders and the paddy fields at sunset is famous.'],
'yantroddharaka':['The image is unusual: Hanumān sits cross-legged in meditation, inside a hexagram, surrounded by a ring of small monkeys holding each other’s tails. Inscriptions on the rock carry the bīja syllables of the yantra.',
 'The story is that Vyāsatīrtha drew Hanumān’s image on a rock each day, and each day it vanished, until he bound it within the yantra.'],
'bengaluru':['Gāli Āñjaneya is one of Bengaluru’s oldest and busiest Hanumān temples, on the Mysore Road at the river crossing. It is linked by tradition to Vyāsatīrtha’s 732 images.',
 'Ragigudda (“millet hill”) temple sits on a granite outcrop in Jayanagar; its name comes from a local story about a heap of millet that turned into a rock. Its Hanumān Jayantī celebrations are among the largest in the city.'],
'namakkal':['The Nāmakkal Āñjaneya is one of the tallest single-stone Hanumān images in South India. He faces Lakṣmī Narasiṃha in the rock-cut cave temple on the opposite side, with joined hands, as if in eternal worship.',
 'The roofless shrine has a legend: attempts to build a roof failed because Hanumān keeps growing. The image is regularly coated with butter and garlanded with thousands of vaḍas.'],
'suchindram':['The Thāṇumālayan temple is dedicated to Śiva, Viṣṇu and Brahmā as one (Sthāṇu–Māl–Ayan). Its Hanumān image is one of the largest in Tamil Nadu and attracts its own crowds.',
 'Local tradition holds the image was buried to protect it during the wars of the 18th century and found again in the 1930s.'],
'kondagattu':['Koṇḍagaṭṭu, “hill” in Telugu, is the main destination for Telangana’s Hanumān dīkṣā pilgrims. Its image shows Āñjaneya with a Narasiṃha face beside his own.',
 'Karmanghat in Hyderabad has a long history; a well-known local story tells how it survived an attempt to destroy it in the 17th century.'],
'panchamukhi':['Pañcamukhi is a short drive from Mantrālayam, where the 17th-century Mādhva saint Rāghavendra Tīrtha entered samādhi. Tradition says he meditated at this rock for years and had a vision of the five-faced Hanumān.',
 'Pilgrims usually combine it with Mantrālayam and nearby Bichāle.'],
'more':['These are only some of the great shrines. Each state has its own famous Hanumān: Kaṣṭabhañjan at Sāraṅgpur in Gujarat for those in trouble; Hanumān Dhārā at Citrakūṭa; giant statues at Paritala in Andhra and many places near Bengaluru.',
 'In Sri Lanka, sites in the central hills, like Rāmboḍa and Seetha Eliya, are linked by local tradition to Hanumān’s search for Sītā.'],
'visit':['A typical Tuesday visit: arrive at dusk, offer sindūr or oil, light a lamp, circle the shrine, recite the Cālīsā and take prasāda. Many devotees vow to visit for 11 or 21 Tuesdays in a row.',
 'The next section of this series moves from places to words: Hanumān’s hymns, verse by verse, starting with the Hanumān Cālīsā.'],
}
hbuild(I,'HN6_Temples','Hanumān’s great temples across India: Saṅkaṭ Mocan (Vārāṇasī), Hanumāngaṛhī (Ayodhyā), Lete Hanumān (Prayāgrāj), Mahāvīr Mandir (Patna), Jakhu (Shimla), Sālāsar and Mehandipur Bālājī, Añjaneri, Añjanādri and Yantroddhāraka (Hampi), Gāli Āñjaneya (Bengaluru), Nāmakkal, Suchindram, Koṇḍagaṭṭu and Pañcamukhi.',
 'HANUMĀN · PART 6 OF 6','HANUMĀN’S TEMPLES',15,'Great shrines across India',art,
 ['Hanumān lives at the edge of every village, but some of his shrines have become great pilgrim centres: Saṅkaṭ Mocan where Tulasīdāsa saw him, Hanumāngaṛhī guarding Ayodhyā, the bearded Bālājī of Sālāsar, the giant Āñjaneya of Nāmakkal and the birthplace hill of Añjanādri across from Hampi.',
  'This part visits the best known, north to south, with their legends and what makes each special.'],
 'Can I visit them all?','Not in one trip. But most of us live near at least one.',pages,EX)
